COPY public.projects (project_id, project_name, project_slug, project_short_description, project_description, "project_githubUrl", "project_liveUrl", "project_thumbnailUrl", project_pictures, project_techstack, project_status, project_created_at, project_updated_at) FROM stdin;
1	stargazer	40d6ed6c-0bf5-4ce5-9ebc-e869639cb132	A demo e-commerce landing page with a dark gothic streetwear aesthetic.	Stargazer is a demo e-commerce site I built for a dark streetwear brand. It's not a real store, just a landing page I made to practice building out a proper storefront layout — hero section, product carousels, brand browsing, the whole thing.\nI wanted something with a bold, gothic aesthetic instead of another generic clean minimal store, so I went with a custom display font, high-contrast black and white, and heavy editorial-style photos. The layout has a hero banner with a "New Arrivals" call to action, scrollable product sections organized by brand, a "Browse by Brand" section, and a footer with newsletter signup and store info.\nMostly built this to practice structuring product grids, carousels, and brand sections while also having fun with the visual design side of things.	https://github.com/yion-dev/stargazer	https://stargazer-m4rr.vercel.app/	https://lruzdrf7t7zl7ff6.public.blob.vercel-storage.com/Projects/project2.webm	{https://lruzdrf7t7zl7ff6.public.blob.vercel-storage.com/Projects/project2.webm}	["React", "TypeScript", "GSAP", "Tailwind"]	completed	2026-07-09 03:44:58.137659+00	2026-07-09 03:44:58.137659+00
2	chipx	b5330e2d-4945-4e86-8990-c33054636bfd	A CHIP-8 emulator built in C using SDL2. 	CHIP-8 emulator in C using SDL2. If you don't know what that is, CHIP-8 is an old interpreted language from the 70s that a bunch of simple games were built on (Tetris, Pong, Space Invaders, etc), and building it forces you to actually understand how a CPU works instead of just knowing it in theory. I learnt a lot from this project, especially around bitwise operations, memory addressing, and what a program counter and stack actually do instead of just being words in a textbook. At one point I spent way too long debugging a bug where my call stack was silently chopping 16-bit addresses down to 8 bits because I typed uint8_t instead of uint16_t in one line, which broke everything and took real log digging to catch. I relied a lot on Cowgod's CHIP-8 technical reference and Columbia University's CHIP-8 design specification PDF to get the instruction set, timing behavior, and overall structure right. Also ended up building a little CLI tool so I could just run chipx <game name> from any terminal on the system instead of dealing with paths every time, which covered some shell scripting and PATH stuff along the way.	https://github.com/yion-dev/chipx	https://github.com/yion-dev/chipx	https://lruzdrf7t7zl7ff6.public.blob.vercel-storage.com/Projects/project3.webm	{https://lruzdrf7t7zl7ff6.public.blob.vercel-storage.com/Projects/project3.webm}	["C", "SDL2", "Make"]	completed	2026-07-09 03:59:44.066447+00	2026-07-09 03:59:44.066447+00
